<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabul is a city in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, strategically placed on routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, the last Timurid prince expelled from Central Asia, took Kabul and used it as his base. From there he invaded India and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, as Timurid rule in Central Asia came to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabul.jpg" alt="Kabul" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Weaveravel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kabul,_Afghanistan_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His first actions were to take Qalati Ghilji and Ghazni from the Ghilji and <b>Kabul</b> and Peshawar from the Mughal governor Nasir Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ruling nominally as a Samanid governor, Sabuktigin (r. 977–997) extended his power over Bust and the <b>Kabul</b> valley and defeated Jayapala, the Hindu Shahi king, pushing his frontier towards Peshawar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Principalities in Chaghaniyan, Khuttal, Termez, Balkh, Badghis, Herat and <b>Kabul</b> held castles on the roads to Bamiyan, where the giant Buddhas were built around 570 and 618.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His coins have been found in Arachosia and the <b>Kabul</b> valley; he conquered those regions and perhaps Gandhara, though he struck no Indian coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kujula then crossed the Hindu Kush, took the <b>Kabul</b> region and Gandhara from the Indo-Parthian successors of Gondophares and absorbed the last Indo-Greek and Saka principalities; some of his early coins imitate those of the Indo-Greek king Hermaeus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad Shaybani Khan's campaigns of 1500–1507 drove the Timurid prince Babur from Samarkand and Transoxiana and extinguished the Herat branch, pushing Babur south to <b>Kabul</b> and ultimately to found the Mughal Empire in India, the final and most enduring expression of the Timurid military tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
